# Code Quality and Minimal Modification Guidelines

## Context

- Applies to all code writing and modification tasks
- Ensures all code changes remain concise and efficient
- Avoids introducing unnecessary complexity and redundancy
- Cultivates the habit of thorough thinking before implementation

## Critical Rules

- Make each modification with minimal changes to meet user requirements
- Maintain extremely high code reusability, prioritize existing functions and libraries
- Do not introduce duplicate, redundant, or unused code
- Think through multiple implementation approaches before actual coding
- Clean and optimize any modified code segments
- Maintain code structure consistency and readability
- Ensure all modifications have clear purpose and value
- Preserve functional integrity of existing features with every change
- Maintain consistent UI/layout patterns throughout modifications
- Design new features with elegance and refinement in mind

## Examples

<example>
// Good code modification example
// Reuse existing function for new requirements
function processItem(item) {
  return transformData(item);
}

// Reuse existing structure when adding new features
function handleNewFeature() {
  const data = fetchData();
  return processItem(data);
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// Poor code modification example
// Duplicating existing functionality
function processItem(item) {
  return transformData(item);
}

// Redundant code, not reusing existing function
function handleNewFeature() {
  const data = fetchData();
  return transformData(data); // Should call processItem
}

// Introducing unused code
function unusedFunction() {
  // Never called function
}
</example> 